$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Procedure B (used for compounds that easily sublime). A mixture
of DMF (0.500 mL, 6.45 mmol) and 10 M KOH (0.500 mL, 5.00 mmol) were
heated at reflux for 5 min. The corresponding aryl halide (1.00 mmol) was
added and the resulting mixture was heated for 0.5 h at 95 °C. The
mixture was treated sequentially with additional DMF (0.500 mL, 6.45
mmol) and 10 M KOH (0.500 mL, 5.00 mmol) from the top of the condenser to
wash down the sublimed aryl halide and heated for an additional 0.5 h.
The reaction was monitored by TLC and additional portions of DMF (0.500
mL, 6.45 mmol) and 10M KOH (0.500 mL, 5.00 mmol) were added in 0.5 h
intervals until completion was evident. The reaction mixture was then
cooled, diluted with water, and extracted with 3 x 10 mL Et2O (unless
otherwise stated). The organic fractions were combined, dried over
anhydrous Na2SO4, and concentrated under reduced pressure. The crude
final product. Method 2 (entry 3): Using General Procedure B,
1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene (0.160 g, 0.120 mL, 1.00 mmol) underwent
conversion after stirring for 2 h at 95 °C. The product crystallized from
the reaction mixture and was collected by vacuum filtration and washed
with ice cold water (10.0 mL). N,N-dimethyl-4-nitrobenzenamine (162 mg,
93%) that when analyzed match 1H NMR spectra from method 1. 1H NMR
(CDCl3, 400 MHz) δ 8.09 (d, J=9.1 Hz, 2H), 6.59 (d, J=9.1 Hz, 2H), 3.10
(s, 6H).[2]
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative preparation shown, incremental addition of KOH and DMF in
0.5h intervals in stage 3
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM General method for nucleophilic aromatic substitution of aryl fluorides
and chlorides with dimethylamine using hydroxide-assisted decomposition
of N,N-dimethylformamide
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Garcia, Juana; Sorrentino, Jacob; Diller, Emily J.; Chapman, Daniel;
Woydziak, Zachary R.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthetic Communications (2016), 46(5), 475-481

